<description>
# A Power Meter on a rectifier with a capacitor

The demo of the **Power Meter** on AC (`docs/POWER_METER.md`): a 10 V peak, 50 Hz sine through a 1N4007 into a 100 µF capacitor and a 1 kΩ load, with the meter in the sine's lead, its red lead toward the generator and `Reading` on Mean.

Run: the meter reads **80.1 mW**, 7.071 V r.m.s., 30.47 mA r.m.s., power factor **0.372**. The capacitor only tops up near the sine's peaks, so the current flows in short pulses (0.31 A at the most) while the voltage is a sine: the apparent power is 215 mVA, of which 80 mW is real. The rest is the reservoir's charging and discharging, back and forth, cycle after cycle: no energy, but current all the same.

`Reading` on Instant would show the power jumping with each pulse. A 100 Ω load instead of 1 kΩ gives a power factor of 0.41; a sine straight into a resistor gives 1.000 (`power_meter_dc.sim1` is the DC case).

| Part | Value | Why |
|---|---|---|
| `AC` | 10 V peak, 50 Hz, `Steps` 20,000 | A smooth sine: at the generator's 100 steps a cycle a capacitor sees a staircase. |
| `PM2` | Power Meter, `Reading` Mean | The new part, in the generator's lead. |
| `D1` | 1N4007 | The rectifier. |
| `C1` | 100 µF | The reservoir capacitor. |
| `R3` | 1 kΩ | The load. |

Open on purpose: `PM2.outnod`, which gives the power as a voltage (1 V = 1 W).

## create-circuit skill

- Version: 1.31.0 (commit 6746f6a)
- Circuit: power_meter_ac.sim1

### Limitations found while building it

None reported.
</description>


